1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:09,216
This video is about aliasing and mutation. Aliasing means two names for one object. Mutation means a change to an object.

2
00:00:09,666 --> 00:00:21,549
We use the object diagrams from the last video. We solve two past exam questions on lists: one mutable, one immutable. At the end we see the keyword final.

3
00:00:21,999 --> 00:00:35,076
The course notes define aliasing: multiple variables all refer to the same object. In Superman's story, at line 35, Clark Kent and Superman both point to Person 3.

4
00:00:35,526 --> 00:00:47,963
The notes say that three names, Kal El, Clark Kent and Superman, refer to Person 3. Be exact about time. Kal El and Clark Kent were aliases after line 28.

5
00:00:48,413 --> 00:01:02,408
Line 31 set Kal El to null. So at line 35 the aliases are Clark Kent and Superman. Aliasing can make fiction more colourful, but it makes programs harder to read and understand.

6
00:01:02,858 --> 00:01:18,623
The notes make the point with Dostoevsky's novel, The Brothers Karamazov. Each brother has many names. Line 5 makes Brother 1. Lines 6 to 9 copy the arrow of Dmitri four times. Five names, one object.

7
00:01:19,073 --> 00:01:26,604
Lines 11 to 14 do the same for the second brother. Ivan and three more names point to Brother 2.

8
00:01:27,054 --> 00:01:35,737
Lines 16 to 23 give the third brother eight names. This time each line copies the previous name, not Alexei.

9
00:01:36,187 --> 00:02:00,379
Look at line 18: Alyoshka equals Alyosha. The right side follows Alyosha's arrow to Brother 3. So Alyoshka points to Brother 3, not to the slot Alyosha. A chain of copies still ends at one object. Three new statements, three objects, seventeen names. This is the object diagram at P C 24 in the notes.

10
00:02:00,829 --> 00:02:17,511
The Brothers Karamazov was made to have many aliases. The next story, Where The Wild Things Are, is a more realistic program. Aliasing comes from procedure calls, and sometimes we add an alias to make the program easier to understand.

11
00:02:17,961 --> 00:02:26,772
Two aliases are added to aid understanding. Line 5: Max In Wolf Suit is Max. Line 19: King is p.

12
00:02:27,222 --> 00:02:36,673
Line 12: the parameter p. A procedure parameter is an alias that you cannot avoid. Every call copies an arrow into p.

13
00:02:37,123 --> 00:02:48,728
And two lines mutate a field: line 13 sets p dot isLonely to true, and line 10 sets Max dot isLonely to false. Who is p? Let us run it.

14
00:02:49,178 --> 00:02:59,802
Lines 3 to 5. Mother is Person 1, and Max is Person 2. Both start with isLonely false. Max In Wolf Suit copies Max's arrow.

15
00:03:00,252 --> 00:03:10,535
Line 8 calls sail Away with Max In Wolf Suit. The new frame has slots p, King and wildthings. p gets the same arrow, to Person 2.

16
00:03:10,985 --> 00:03:21,203
Line 13: p dot isLonely equals true. Follow p to Person 2. So this line changes Max's object, although it never says Max.

17
00:03:21,653 --> 00:03:31,787
Lines 14 and 15 make an array and three Wild Things. The slot wildthings points to the array, and the array points to the three objects.

18
00:03:32,237 --> 00:03:44,631
Line 18: Person 2 can stare without blinking, so line 19 runs. King equals p. Now Max, Max In Wolf Suit, p and King are four names for Person 2.

19
00:03:45,081 --> 00:03:54,489
King dot isLonely is true, so the program reaches line 32. This is the object diagram at P C 32 in the notes.

20
00:03:54,939 --> 00:04:04,134
Mutation means a change to an object. Line 13 mutates p dot isLonely. Line 10 mutates Max dot isLonely.

21
00:04:04,584 --> 00:04:12,285
The reader of the story knows that both lines change the same object, Person 2. p and Max are aliases.

22
00:04:12,735 --> 00:04:25,386
This is hard for the compiler to know. It must do an inter procedural pointer analysis. That is our object diagram, but built for every program point and every possible input.

23
00:04:25,836 --> 00:04:34,689
It is even harder for a programmer in a large program that is not a famous story. Be careful about mutation and aliasing.

24
00:04:35,139 --> 00:04:52,953
Now a past exam question, from the slides and questions pre 2.1.1. The program uses a linked list from the J D K, the Java Development Kit. The J D K lists are mutable. What does line 10 print? Commit to one answer.

25
00:04:53,403 --> 00:04:55,045
Pause the video and try it.

26
00:04:55,495 --> 00:05:00,914
Run it by hand. Line 6: new makes Linked List 1, and list points to it.

27
00:05:01,364 --> 00:05:08,809
Line 7: alias equals list. No new, so no new bubble. alias points to the same Linked List 1.

28
00:05:09,259 --> 00:05:24,321
Line 8: list dot add hello. The add method mutates Linked List 1. It makes a node inside the list, and the node refers to the string hello. The new for the node is inside the library code of add.

29
00:05:24,771 --> 00:05:33,219
Line 9: alias dot add world. alias leads to the same object, so the same list gets a second node, with world.

30
00:05:33,669 --> 00:05:44,314
Line 10 prints the list: hello, world. The answer is c. Lines 8 and 9 both mutate one object, because list and alias are aliases.

31
00:05:44,764 --> 00:05:52,188
Mutable means can be changed. Immutable means cannot be changed. The J D K lists are mutable.

32
00:05:52,638 --> 00:06:01,470
Parboiled is the parser generator that the labs use later. It comes with a class called Immutable List. Start with an empty one.

33
00:06:01,920 --> 00:06:12,587
To add an item, you call append. Append does not change the list. It makes a new list with the old contents and the new item. The old list stays empty.

34
00:06:13,037 --> 00:06:32,749
Why would compiler engineers want this? Procedure calls make aliases that you cannot avoid, and a compiler has many calls. Immutable data makes the code easier to reason about, gives fewer bugs, and makes debugging easier. The trade off is that adding an item feels different.

35
00:06:33,199 --> 00:06:50,351
The second exam question, from the slides and questions pre 2.1.2. The same program shape, but with Parboiled's Immutable List and append. What does line 9 print? And what does line 12 print? Each has the same four options.

36
00:06:50,801 --> 00:06:52,443
Pause the video and try it.

37
00:06:52,893 --> 00:07:02,749
Lines 5 and 6. Immutable List dot of makes an empty list, Immutable List 1. list points to it, and alias copies the arrow.

38
00:07:03,199 --> 00:07:18,218
Line 7: list dot append hello. Append makes a new list, Immutable List 2, with hello. But the program does not assign the result. No slot points to Immutable List 2. List 1 does not change.

39
00:07:18,668 --> 00:07:25,004
Line 8 does the same with alias. It makes Immutable List 3 with world, and throws it away.

40
00:07:25,454 --> 00:07:35,587
Line 9 prints list. list still points to Immutable List 1, which is empty. The output is the empty list. The answer for line 9 is a.

41
00:07:36,037 --> 00:07:52,400
Line 10: list equals list dot append hello. Append makes Immutable List 4 with hello. It refers to the same String 1. This time we assign the result: erase the old arrow of list and draw it to Immutable List 4.

42
00:07:52,850 --> 00:08:12,306
Line 11: alias equals alias dot append world. Resolve alias first. It still points to Immutable List 1, the empty list. So append makes Immutable List 5 with only world. alias now points to it. list and alias are no longer aliases.

43
00:08:12,756 --> 00:08:28,052
Line 12 prints list: hello. The answer for line 12 is b, not hello world. Each name got its own new list. Nothing was shared, so nothing was mutated behind our back. We checked both outputs in Java.

44
00:08:28,502 --> 00:08:34,966
The notes also show the right way. Line 5: list points to the empty Immutable List 1.

45
00:08:35,416 --> 00:08:43,459
Line 6: list equals list dot append hello. The new Immutable List 2 holds hello, and list moves to it.

46
00:08:43,909 --> 00:08:52,762
Line 7: append world to Immutable List 2. The new Immutable List 3 holds hello and world, and list moves to it.

47
00:08:53,212 --> 00:09:02,727
Line 8 prints hello, world. With an immutable list, always assign the result of append. The old lists stay unchanged in the heap.

48
00:09:03,177 --> 00:09:19,198
Last topic: the keyword final. In Java, final prevents re-assignment. When you write final, the compiler must prove that the variable is assigned exactly once on every program path. A parameter can also be final.

49
00:09:19,648 --> 00:09:26,923
msg is declared final on line 7, without a value. If args is empty, line 8 assigns it.

50
00:09:27,373 --> 00:09:29,549
Otherwise, line 9 assigns it.

51
00:09:29,999 --> 00:09:51,801
Both paths meet at line 10, and on each path msg was assigned exactly once. So javac accepts it. The field f of class C is final too: the compiler proves that every constructor assigns it exactly once. After the midterm, we learn how a compiler makes this proof, with dataflow analysis.

52
00:09:52,251 --> 00:10:12,049
Here is the object diagram at P C 11, with no command line arguments. s points to the string known. msg points to no args. c points to C 1, and its field f points to the same string. The notes also draw the literal some args. No arrow points to it.

53
00:10:12,499 --> 00:10:21,245
Line 11 prints c dot f: no args. Every slot here was set in exactly one place, and never changed after.

54
00:10:21,695 --> 00:10:37,226
Now a wrong use, from the notes. Line 3 sets the final s, and line 4 tries to assign s again. javac refuses: cannot assign a value to final variable s. A final slot keeps its first arrow.

55
00:10:37,676 --> 00:10:48,407
Compare the two ideas. final is about a slot: its arrow is set once and never moves. Immutable is about an object: its contents never change.

56
00:10:48,857 --> 00:11:03,043
So final alone does not stop mutation. Our example: a final list variable, and then list dot add hello. It compiles, and it prints hello. The arrow did not move, but the object changed.

57
00:11:03,493 --> 00:11:16,443
The notes combine the two. With an immutable data structure and final, a value is set in exactly one place and does not change after that. This makes the program easier to reason about.

58
00:11:16,893 --> 00:11:26,215
One more note from the course notes: Java final is similar to, but not exactly the same as, the C and C plus plus keyword const.

59
00:11:26,665 --> 00:11:35,007
To summarize. Aliasing means several names point to one object. A copy follows the arrow, and never points to a slot.

60
00:11:35,457 --> 00:11:43,435
Procedure parameters are aliases that you cannot avoid. In the Wild Things story, p and Max are one object.

61
00:11:43,885 --> 00:11:51,800
A mutation through one alias is seen through all of them. That is why the linked list program prints hello, world.

62
00:11:52,250 --> 00:12:01,530
An immutable append makes a new list. Assign the result, or it is lost. The Parboiled program prints the empty list, then hello.

63
00:12:01,980 --> 00:12:15,185
final means assigned exactly once on every path, and the compiler proves it. It fixes the slot, not the object. Practice these diagrams. Object diagrams are midterm material.
